Course 1: Control and first touch (Detailed Research)

Category: Technique
Duration: 25 min
Level: 2/5
What the Course Should Cover

This course focuses on oriented receiving — not just stopping the ball, but using the first touch to move the ball into space and set up the next action. The key principle is that the first touch should always have a purpose: to escape pressure, to face forward, or to create a passing angle.
Key Coaching Points
Body Orientation Before Receiving

    Open up your body before the ball arrives so you can receive on either foot and see the field

    Check your shoulders constantly to scan the space behind and around you

    Get on your toes and be ready to adjust your feet quickly as the ball approaches

    Time your movement so you arrive as the ball arrives, not before or after 

The First Touch Itself

    Take your first touch into space — away from pressure and toward where you want to go

    Use a "soft" or "cushioned" touch — relax the controlling surface at the moment of contact so the ball doesn't bounce away

    Direct the ball with the first touch to set up your next action (pass, shot, or dribble)

    Avoid a heavy touch that forces a second touch to get the ball out of your feet 

Decision-Making Under Pressure

    Receive side-on so you stay facing forward and can play in any direction

    Use your body and arms to protect the ball from defenders behind you

    Recognize when to play forward versus when to shield and retain possession 

Common Mistakes to Address
Mistake	Why It Happens	Correction
Ball bounces off player	Tensing up, stiff controlling surface	Relax the foot/thigh on contact — "catch it like an egg"
Player needs a second touch to get ball out of feet	Poor first touch direction	Take first touch into space, not straight down
Player gets caught in possession	Receiving facing own goal or with closed body	Open body before receiving, check shoulders early
Indecisive about which surface to use	Lack of repetition	Practice receiving with inside, outside, sole, thigh, chest
Sample Drills
Drill 1: Wall Passing (Solo)

    Stand 3-5 meters from a solid wall

    Pass the ball at different heights

    Control each rebound with a different surface: instep, outside of foot, thigh, chest

    Aim for 50 clean touches per session 

Drill 2: Cone Receive and Turn

    Set up four cones in a square about 5 meters apart

    Toss or kick the ball against the wall

    Receive it and immediately turn toward the next cone

    Combines first touch with movement 

Drill 3: Oriented Control with Pressure (Pairs)

    Player A passes to Player B

    Player B must take their first touch through a gate or cone before passing back

    Add a passive defender applying pressure from behind to simulate match conditions 

Drill 4: 2v2 Transition Game

    Small-sided game emphasizing receiving under pressure

    Players must take a good first touch that sets them up to pass or shoot

    Coach counts down from 5 to encourage quick decisions 

Progression Variables

    Surface variety: Inside, outside, sole, thigh, chest

    Service variety: Ground passes, bouncing balls, aerial balls

    Pressure level: No pressure → passive defender → active defender

    Direction demand: Receive and turn → receive and pass → receive and shoot

    Space restriction: Larger area → smaller area to increase decision speed 

Assessment Indicators

A player has improved when they:

    Take their first touch away from pressure without needing a second touch to control

    Scan before receiving consistently (checking shoulders)

    Receive side-on and can play forward immediately

    Adjust their body to receive on either foot based on the situation

    Retain possession in 1v1 or 2v1 situations more frequently 

Session Structure Suggestion (25 minutes)
Time	Activity	Focus
5 min	Dynamic warm-up with ball	Ball familiarity, juggling, different surfaces
8 min	Technical drill (wall passing or cone turns)	Isolated first-touch repetition
7 min	Pressure drill (pairs with passive defender)	Applying technique under light pressure
5 min	Small-sided game (2v2 or 3v3)	Transfer to game-realistic situations
